[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Classes and Objects in PHP: A Comprehensive Guide"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"PHP is an object-oriented language, meaning it supports the creation and manipulation of objects. This tutorial will guide you through the fundamentals of classes and objects in PHP, covering defining classes and instantiating objects."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining Classes"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A class is a blueprint for creating objects. It defines the properties (data) and methods (functions) that objects of that class will have. The basic syntax for defining a class in PHP is as follows:"},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"<?php
class ClassName {
  // Properties
  public $property1;
  private $property2;
  protected $property3;

  // Methods
  public function method1() {
    // Code here
  }
  private function method2() {
    // Code here
  }
  protected function method3() {
    // Code here
  }
}
?>"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The `class` keyword is used to declare a new class."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`ClassName` is the name of the class. Choose a descriptive name that reflects the purpose of the class."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Properties are variables that hold data. They are declared using keywords like `public`, `private`, or `protected`, which control their visibility."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Methods are functions that define the behavior of the class. They are also declared with visibility keywords (`public`, `private`, `protected`)."},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let's look at a more concrete example:"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"<?php
class Car {
  public $color;
  public $model;

  public function __construct($color, $model) {
    $this->color = $color;
    $this->model = $model;
  }

  public function drive() {
    return "The car is driving!";
  }
}
?>"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `Car` class has two properties (`color` and `model`) and two methods (`__construct` and `drive`). The `__construct` method is a special method called a constructor, which is automatically called when a new object of the class is created. It initializes the object's properties."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Instantiating Objects"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once you have defined a class, you can create objects of that class. This process is called instantiation. To instantiate an object, you use the `new` keyword:"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"<?php
$myCar = new Car("red", "Toyota");

echo $myCar->color; // Output: red
echo $myCar->model; // Output: Toyota
echo $myCar->drive(); // Output: The car is driving!
?>"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The `new` keyword creates a new object of the `Car` class."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The arguments passed to `new Car()` are passed to the constructor (`__construct`) method."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The `->` operator is used to access the properties and methods of the object."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Visibility (Public, Private, Protected)"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"PHP uses visibility keywords to control access to class properties and methods:"},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`public`: Properties and methods declared as `public` can be accessed from anywhere (inside the class, outside the class, and by child classes)."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`private`: Properties and methods declared as `private` can only be accessed from within the class that defines them. They are not accessible from outside the class or by child classes."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`protected`: Properties and methods declared as `protected` can be accessed from within the class that defines them and by child classes (classes that inherit from the parent class). They are not accessible from outside the class."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"<?php
class Animal {
  public $name;
  private $sound;
  protected $type;

  public function __construct($name, $sound, $type) {
    $this->name = $name;
    $this->sound = $sound;
    $this->type = $type;
  }

  public function makeSound() {
    return $this->sound; // Accessing private property within the class
  }

  protected function getType() {
    return $this->type; // Accessing protected property within the class
  }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  public function displayType() {
    return $this->getType(); // Accessing protected property from child class
  }
}

$animal = new Animal("Generic Animal", "Generic Sound", "Mammal");
echo $animal->name . "\n"; // OK: public property
// echo $animal->sound; // Fatal error: Cannot access private property

$dog = new Dog("Buddy", "Woof", "Canine");
echo $dog->displayType() . "\n"; // OK: Accessing protected property via child class method
?>"},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial has covered the basics of classes and objects in PHP, including defining classes, instantiating objects, and understanding visibility. Mastering these concepts is crucial for writing well-structured and maintainable PHP code. Remember to practice and experiment with different examples to solidify your understanding."}]
